29.2 Exact restoration under T1
With the same T1 on every qubit, the filter returns exactly the noiseless readout in every sector (Chapter 21, checked to \(10^{-12}\) m-qg). In classification, the notes (§80) measured it over 60 paired runs (5 seeds, 3 splits, 4 datasets), with predictions committed before running:

with the weight-1 model, trained without noise and read under T1, qang recovers +2.5 points of accuracy (95% interval: +0.8 to +4.1), up to the exact accuracy of 0.952;
with the weight-2 model it recovers +16.5 points (+11.8 to +21.2), from 0.757 to 0.922;
with dephasing, +12.6 and +22.6 points;
when training under the noise, the difference vanishes (+0.1 and −0.1 points): the advantage is not having to train under the noise, not an accuracy higher than achievable.

All five predictions held. The index’s figures (“+2.5 and +16.5 over 60 runs”) are in §80 as stated.

30.2 Graph states: entanglement invisible in Z
A graph state is \(H^{\otimes n}\) followed by one CZ per edge. CZ is diagonal, so it only changes phases: every Z population stays at \(2^{-n}\). The script checks it on the 6-qubit ring:

its Z distribution matches that of \(|+\rangle^{\otimes 6}\), which has no entanglement, with a difference of exactly 0;
so \(qg_Z = 0\) on every qubit, \(qg_S = 1\) and the qg correlation is 0 for both states;
the stabilizers \(X_i Z_{i-1} Z_{i+1}\) are 1 on the graph state and 0 on the product. They tell the two states apart, but they are measured in the X basis.

The same happens without qang: any Z-basis statistic, raw or tomographic, is blind to this entanglement. qang inherits the limit, it does not create it (notes §8.1). Readout dephasing is the same blind spot seen from the noise side (§10.4).

the weight filter, if the circuit conserves the weight;
the witness that says how much the filter removed (the kept fraction);
the readout in m-qg, where a probability difference of 0.0005 is 1 m-qg.

an SDK that writes qubit 0 on the right and another that writes it on the left swap the qubits without an error;
a primitive that returns \(\langle Z_i\rangle\) already gives \(qg_Z\), but not the shots the filter needs to discard;
a compiler that picks bad qubits lowers the kept fraction before anything is measured.

a reading: turn what the library already measures (counts, populations, detectors, probabilities) into \(qg_Z\) and m-qg, with a single qubit order (Chapter 32);
the filter: keep the shots in the circuit’s Hamming-weight sector, with the kept fraction \(K\) (Chapters 21 and 22);
a closed-form rule: the filter’s cost rule (Chapter 21), the closed form of heralded calibration (§31) or the minimal pulse area (§160).

35.9 SDKs: Qibo, pytket, Braket and CUDA-Q
Each gets the qang rotation (\(R_Y(\arccos qg)\)) and the canonical reading with the filter:

Qibo: the reading of \(qg=0.6\) with 100,000 shots gives 0.5978;
pytket: gives the exact value, \(-0.25\), from the statevector. pytket measures angles in half-turns, not radians, and rqang does the conversion. It is the kind of unit error this scale is meant to prevent;
Braket: runs on the LocalSimulator, so it is no longer just a result format (Chapter 32); the reading of 0.2 gives 0.201;
CUDA-Q: runs in WSL with CUDA-Q 0.16.0. On the CPU simulator (qpp-cpu), the reading of \(-0.5\) gives \(-0.5018\) and the filter keeps 0.7509, against 0.75 expected. The GPU simulator (nvidia, cuStateVec) did not start, because the CUDA driver WSL sees is older than CUDA-Q’s runtime. The Windows NVIDIA driver needs updating; the code does not change, only cudaq.set_target("nvidia").

35.11 Environment conflicts
Putting everything in one environment was not possible, and the conflicts are information:

Qiskit Dynamics 0.6 requires qiskit&lt;=1.3;
PennyLane 0.45 does not work with JAX ≥ 0.9;
amazon-braket-sdk pins cloudpickle==2.2.1, while recent joblib (used by scikit-learn) needs ≥ 3.0. Both worked with cloudpickle 3.1.2, but pip warns about the conflict;
mitiq 1.1 pulled NumPy down to 2.2.6;
CUDA-Q has no wheels for Windows and was tested in WSL; its GPU simulator needs a newer NVIDIA driver.

That is why there are three environments: .venv-int with almost everything, .venv-dyn for Qiskit Dynamics and WSL for CUDA-Q. The book’s .venv was left untouched.
